{"text":"MODEL_FEEDBACK_JSON:
{
  "positive_alignment_reasons": [
    "Aenima and Lateralus feel like natural next choices after 10.000 Days because I want more of Tool's heavy riffs, complex rhythms, and immersive arrangements.",
    "American Idiot fits the accessible side of my listening through energetic guitars and direct hooks; The Young and the Hopeless has some of that appeal too, though I would choose the heavier albums first."
  ],
  "negative_misalignment_reasons": [
    "X&Y and How to Dismantle An Atomic share a broad rock connection with my listening, but their mellower or anthemic sound does not give me the weight and intensity I want now.",
    "Kid A's atmospheric electronics, The Marshall Mathers' rap, No Strings Attached's polished pop, and The Piper at the Gates of Dawn's psychedelic sound miss my current desire for heavy guitars and forceful rock vocals."
  ],
  "overgeneralized_signals": [
    "The choices seem to treat a broad connection to rock as enough, even though heaviness and rhythmic intensity make a big difference to me today.",
    "The accessible hooks in part of my history seem to have been stretched into an interest in softer rock and polished pop, when I still want those hooks backed by strong guitars."
  ],
  "missing_or_underweighted_signals": [
    "My recent listening to 10.000 Days makes more Tool especially compelling, including the intricate arrangements as well as the heavy sound.",
    "Albums such as Ten Thousand Fists and my earlier heavier listening suggest that intensity matters; a catchy rock album can appeal without being my first choice right now."
  ],
  "summary_diagnosis": "The recommendations understood my interest in Tool and energetic rock, but stretched that interest too broadly beyond the heaviness and complexity I want now."
}"}
